/* ============ CONTEMPORARY DEBATES ============ */
const DEBATES=[
 {id:"neuro",title:"The ethics of neuroscience",sideA:"Neuroscience is ethical and beneficial",sideB:"Neuroscience raises serious ethical problems",
  core:"Brain scanning (fMRI, PET), drug research and brain stimulation have transformed our understanding of behaviour, but they raise questions about privacy, free will, responsibility and how the findings are used.",
  args:[["Brain scans help diagnose conditions such as tumours, dementia and stroke","A"],["Research can lead to new treatments such as deep brain stimulation for Parkinson’s disease","A"],["Non-invasive scans such as fMRI use no radiation","A"],["Understanding the brain can reduce stigma by showing mental illness has a biological basis","A"],["Raine et al. (1997) helped show how brain dysfunction may contribute to violence","A"],
   ["Scans could be used to ‘read’ thoughts, threatening privacy","B"],["Neuroscience evidence in court may let offenders avoid responsibility","B"],["Incidental findings, such as an unexpected tumour, can cause distress to volunteers","B"],["Cognitive-enhancing drugs could create unfair advantages","B"],["Biological explanations may ignore free will and social causes","B"]],
  imps:[["Who should have access to a person’s brain data (privacy)","eth"],["Gaining informed consent from vulnerable groups such as offenders","eth"],["Deciding whether to tell volunteers about incidental findings","eth"],
   ["Scanners cost millions of pounds to buy and run","eco"],["Effective early diagnosis could save the NHS money on long-term care","eco"],["Neuromarketing could be used by companies to increase profits","eco"],
   ["Courts may change how responsibility and punishment are judged","soc"],["People labelled with ‘abnormal’ brains could face prejudice","soc"],["Biological explanations may reduce stigma of mental illness","soc"]],
  essay:[
   ["Introduction","Neuroscience is the scientific study of the brain and nervous system. Techniques such as PET and fMRI scanning, drug research and brain stimulation allow psychologists to link behaviour to brain structure and function. This is central to the biological approach, which assumes that behaviour can be explained by localisation of brain function and neurotransmitters. However, the ability to look inside the brain raises ethical questions about privacy, consent, free will and how findings are used in society. This essay will argue both sides before reaching a conclusion."],
   ["Benefits of neuroscience","One argument that neuroscience is ethical is that its benefits to society are enormous. Brain scans allow doctors to diagnose tumours, strokes and dementia earlier, and research has led to treatments such as deep brain stimulation, which can greatly reduce the tremors of Parkinson’s disease and help some patients with treatment-resistant OCD. Drug therapies based on neurotransmitter research, such as SSRIs for depression, help millions of people. Using a cost–benefit analysis, the reduction in suffering arguably outweighs the risks to participants. There are also economic implications: earlier diagnosis and effective treatment may reduce the long-term cost of care to the NHS and allow people to return to work."],
   ["Reducing stigma","A second argument is that neuroscience can reduce stigma. If conditions such as schizophrenia or depression are shown to have a biological basis, the individual may be seen as unwell rather than weak or to blame. This has positive social implications because it may encourage people to seek treatment. Many modern techniques, such as fMRI, are also non-invasive and use no radiation, which lowers the risk of physical harm to participants."],
   ["Privacy and consent","On the other hand, neuroscience raises serious ethical problems. Brain data are highly personal, and as technology improves there are concerns that scans could be used to ‘read’ thoughts, detect lies or predict future behaviour. This threatens a person’s right to privacy and confidentiality. Research also often involves vulnerable participants. In Raine, Buchsbaum and LaCasse (1997), 41 murderers pleading not guilty by reason of insanity were given PET scans, which required a radioactive tracer. It can be questioned whether people in such a position can give fully valid consent. There is also the problem of incidental findings: a healthy volunteer may discover an abnormality they did not expect, which can cause great anxiety."],
   ["Free will and responsibility","A further problem is the social implication for responsibility. Raine et al. found lower glucose metabolism in the prefrontal cortex of murderers, but they warned that this does not show that biology alone causes violence. If brain evidence is used in court as a ‘my brain made me do it’ defence, offenders might avoid responsibility, while others could be labelled as dangerous on the basis of a scan before they have done anything wrong. This conflicts with the positive approach, which acknowledges free will, and it is reductionist, ignoring the childhood experiences emphasised by the psychodynamic approach and the learning emphasised by behaviourists."],
   ["Economic and social costs","There are also economic issues. Scanners cost millions of pounds to buy and run, and money spent on neuroscience research might be used for cheaper talking therapies. Some companies use ‘neuromarketing’ to increase sales, which could be seen as manipulation. Cognitive-enhancing drugs raise questions of fairness if only wealthier students can access them."],
   ["Conclusion","In conclusion, neuroscience is not unethical in itself; the debate is about how it is carried out and used. Its benefits for diagnosis and treatment are great, but researchers must protect privacy, gain valid consent, plan for incidental findings and avoid overstating results. Ethics committees and guidelines, such as those of the British Psychological Society, are essential. Neuroscience is most ethical when its findings are combined with other approaches rather than used to reduce people to their brains."]]},
 {id:"mother",title:"The mother as primary care-giver of an infant",sideA:"The mother should be the primary care-giver",sideB:"Others can be the primary care-giver just as well",
  core:"Bowlby argued that infants need one main attachment figure, usually the mother, for healthy development. Others argue that fathers, grandparents or other carers can provide equally good care.",
  args:[["Bowlby’s monotropy theory says infants form one special attachment, usually to the mother","A"],["Bowlby (1944) linked early maternal separation to affectionless psychopathy","A"],["Evolutionary explanations suggest mothers are biologically prepared through pregnancy and breastfeeding","A"],["Oxytocin released during birth and breastfeeding promotes bonding","A"],["Continuity of care from one person may give the child a secure base","A"],
   ["Schaffer and Emerson (1964) found most babies had multiple attachments by 18 months","B"],["Attachment forms with the most sensitive and responsive carer, not necessarily the mother","B"],["Field (1978) found fathers who were primary carers behaved much like mothers","B"],["Bowlby’s 44 thieves study used retrospective interviews and may be biased","B"],["Good-quality day care does not seem to harm most children","B"]],
  imps:[["Mothers may feel guilty for returning to work","eth"],["Telling fathers they are less important could be socially sensitive","eth"],["Research on separation can involve distressing experiences for families","eth"],
   ["Mothers staying at home may lose income and career progression","eco"],["Shared parental leave and childcare policies cost governments and employers money","eco"],["Families may rely on grandparents or nurseries to afford to work","eco"],
   ["Traditional gender roles may be reinforced","soc"],["Single fathers and same-sex couples can be seen as equally able parents","soc"],["Government policies on early years care (e.g. Flying Start in Wales) are shaped by attachment research","soc"]],
  essay:[
   ["Introduction","A primary care-giver is the person who provides most of an infant’s day-to-day care and to whom the infant forms its strongest attachment. Traditionally this has been the mother, and Bowlby’s work in the 1940s and 1950s argued that a warm, continuous relationship with the mother was essential for mental health. Today, many families share childcare between parents, grandparents and nurseries, so the debate is whether the mother needs to be the primary care-giver at all."],
   ["Bowlby and the psychodynamic view","The strongest argument for the mother comes from Bowlby, who was influenced by the psychodynamic approach’s emphasis on childhood experiences. In his study of 44 juvenile thieves (1944), 14 thieves were classed as affectionless, and 12 of these had experienced prolonged separation from their mother before the age of five. Only 2 of the 44 control children had been separated. Bowlby concluded that maternal deprivation could cause lasting emotional damage. His later theory of monotropy argued that infants form one special attachment, usually with the mother, which acts as an internal working model for later relationships."],
   ["Biological support","The biological approach also gives some support. From an evolutionary view, mothers invest heavily through pregnancy and breastfeeding, so it may be adaptive for infants to attach to them. Hormones such as oxytocin, released in childbirth and breastfeeding, promote bonding. This suggests mothers may be naturally prepared for the role."],
   ["Multiple attachments","However, there is strong evidence that the mother is not the only possible primary care-giver. Schaffer and Emerson (1964) studied 60 babies from Glasgow and found that by 18 months most had multiple attachments, and that babies attached most strongly to the person who was most sensitive and responsive to them, not necessarily the person who fed them most. Field (1978) found that fathers who were primary care-givers smiled at and imitated their babies in a similar way to mothers, suggesting that sensitive caring depends on the role, not on gender. The behaviourist approach would also argue that attachment is learnt through association with food and comfort, so anyone who provides these can become the attachment figure."],
   ["Problems with Bowlby’s evidence","Bowlby’s own evidence has been criticised. The 44 thieves study used retrospective interviews, which may be inaccurate, and Bowlby carried out the interviews himself, which risks researcher bias. It shows a correlation between separation and delinquency, not cause and effect; other factors such as family conflict or poverty may explain the link."],
   ["Implications","The debate has important implications. Socially, insisting that the mother must be the primary care-giver can reinforce traditional gender roles and imply that single fathers or same-sex couples are less able parents. Ethically, it may make working mothers feel guilty. Economically, if mothers stay at home, families lose income and women’s careers suffer, while shared parental leave and childcare support cost governments and employers money. On the other hand, the research led to positive changes such as allowing parents to stay with children in hospital and to early years programmes, like Flying Start in Wales, that support parents."],
   ["Conclusion","In conclusion, research shows that infants need at least one consistent, sensitive and responsive care-giver, but there is little evidence that this must be the mother. The quality of care seems more important than the gender of the person giving it. A balanced view is that mothers often are the primary care-giver for biological and practical reasons, but fathers and others can fulfil the role equally well."]]},
 {id:"condition",title:"Using conditioning techniques to control the behaviour of children",sideA:"Conditioning children is effective and acceptable",sideB:"Conditioning children is unethical or ineffective",
  core:"Parents, schools and therapists use rewards, sanctions, token economies and time out, based on operant conditioning, to shape children’s behaviour. The debate is whether this is a fair and effective way to guide children.",
  args:[["Positive reinforcement such as praise and stickers increases desired behaviour","A"],["Token economies reduce disruptive behaviour in classrooms","A"],["Clear, consistent consequences help children learn boundaries","A"],["Techniques are cheap and easy for parents and teachers to use","A"],["Rewards can replace physical punishment, which is now banned in Wales","A"],
   ["Children may behave well only while rewards are offered","B"],["Lepper et al. (1973) found rewards reduced children’s interest in drawing (overjustification)","B"],["Conditioning ignores free will and the child’s understanding of why a behaviour is wrong","B"],["Punishment can cause fear, anxiety or aggression","B"],["It may be seen as manipulating children who cannot consent","B"]],
  imps:[["Children cannot give consent to being conditioned","eth"],["Punishment can cause psychological harm","eth"],["Children’s autonomy and free will may be ignored","eth"],
   ["Behaviour charts and rewards cost very little","eco"],["Less classroom disruption may raise attainment","eco"],["Intensive programmes such as applied behaviour analysis can be expensive","eco"],
   ["Children may become dependent on external rewards","soc"],["The Welsh ban on physical punishment (2022) changed how parents discipline","soc"],["Consistent behaviour policies may create calmer schools","soc"]],
  essay:[
   ["Introduction","The behaviourist approach assumes that behaviour is learnt through conditioning. Operant conditioning (Skinner) suggests that behaviour followed by reinforcement is repeated, while behaviour followed by punishment is less likely to be repeated. Parents and schools use these ideas every day through praise, star charts, token economies, loss of privileges and time out. The debate is whether using conditioning to control children’s behaviour is effective and ethical."],
   ["Effectiveness","The main argument for conditioning is that it works. Positive reinforcement, such as praise, stickers or golden time, increases desirable behaviour, and token economies have been found to reduce disruptive behaviour in classrooms and residential settings. Negative punishment, such as time out, removes attention that may be reinforcing bad behaviour. Because the techniques are simple and consistent, children learn clear boundaries, which may create a calmer and safer environment for everyone."],
   ["Practical and economic benefits","There are economic advantages too. Behaviour charts and verbal praise cost almost nothing, and if classroom disruption falls, teachers spend more time teaching, which may improve attainment. Reward-based approaches can also replace physical punishment. In Wales, the Children (Abolition of Defence of Reasonable Punishment) (Wales) Act 2020 made smacking illegal from 2022, so parents need effective alternatives, and positive reinforcement is one."],
   ["Short-term compliance","On the other hand, conditioning may only produce short-term compliance. Once rewards stop, the behaviour may undergo extinction. Lepper, Greene and Nisbett (1973) found that nursery children who expected a reward for drawing later spent less free time drawing than children who received no reward, suggesting that rewards can undermine intrinsic motivation (the overjustification effect). This suggests children might learn to behave for the reward rather than because they value the behaviour."],
   ["Ethical concerns","There are also ethical concerns. Children cannot give informed consent to being conditioned, and deliberately shaping their behaviour can be seen as manipulation that ignores free will, which the positive approach emphasises. Punishment can cause fear and anxiety, and the social learning view suggests that physical punishment models aggression. The cognitive approach would argue that children also need to understand why a behaviour is right or wrong; Kohlberg suggested that moral reasoning develops through stages, so simply rewarding obedience may keep children at a low level of moral thinking."],
   ["Social implications","Socially, conditioning may create children who depend on external rewards and who behave well only when watched. It may also be applied unequally, with some groups punished more than others. However, used well, it can give children a positive experience of school and help those with behavioural difficulties."],
   ["Conclusion","In conclusion, conditioning techniques are effective in the short term, cheap and, when based on positive reinforcement, can be ethical. However, they should be used alongside explanation, so that children understand and internalise good behaviour, rather than relying on rewards and punishments alone. Physical punishment should be avoided, and children’s rights and autonomy should be respected."]]},
 {id:"ewt",title:"Reliability of eye-witness testimony (including children)",sideA:"Eye-witness testimony is reliable",sideB:"Eye-witness testimony is unreliable",
  core:"Eye-witness testimony (EWT) is evidence given by a person who saw a crime. Juries find it convincing, but cognitive research suggests memory is reconstructive and can be distorted, especially in children.",
  args:[["Yuille and Cutshall (1986) found real witnesses to a shooting were accurate months later","A"],["Real witnesses may resist misleading questions more than lab participants","A"],["The cognitive interview improves the amount of accurate recall","A"],["Children can give accurate accounts when interviewed carefully with open questions","A"],["Lab studies such as Loftus and Palmer lack ecological validity","A"],
   ["Loftus and Palmer (1974) showed leading questions change estimates of speed","B"],["Post-event information can create false memories such as broken glass","B"],["Weapon focus and high anxiety can reduce the accuracy of identification","B"],["Young children are especially suggestible to repeated or leading questions","B"],["Mistaken eyewitness identification was a factor in around 70% of DNA exoneration cases in the US","B"]],
  imps:[["Innocent people may be wrongly convicted","eth"],["Child witnesses may be distressed by repeated interviews","eth"],["Research using deception about a ‘crime’ raises ethical issues","eth"],
   ["Wrongful convictions and appeals are expensive for the justice system","eco"],["Training police in the cognitive interview costs time and money","eco"],["Video-recording child interviews requires equipment and specialist staff","eco"],
   ["Public trust in the justice system can be damaged","soc"],["Judges warn juries about identification evidence (Turnbull guidelines)","soc"],["Guidance on interviewing children has changed police practice","soc"]],
  essay:[
   ["Introduction","Eye-witness testimony (EWT) is the evidence given in court by a person who has seen a crime. Juries often find it very persuasive. However, the cognitive approach sees memory as an internal mental process that is reconstructive, influenced by schemas and by information received after the event. This essay considers whether EWT, including that of children, can be trusted."],
   ["Evidence that EWT is unreliable","The best-known evidence against EWT is Loftus and Palmer (1974). In Experiment 1, 45 students watched film clips of car crashes and were asked how fast the cars were going when they ‘smashed’, ‘collided’, ‘bumped’, ‘hit’ or ‘contacted’ each other. Those given ‘smashed’ estimated a mean of 40.8 mph compared with 31.8 mph for ‘contacted’. In Experiment 2, 16 of 50 people in the ‘smashed’ group falsely remembered broken glass a week later, compared with 7 in the ‘hit’ group and 6 in the control group. This suggests a leading question can alter the memory itself, supporting the idea that memory is reconstructive."],
   ["Other factors","Other factors can reduce accuracy. Weapon focus may draw attention away from the culprit’s face, and very high anxiety may impair recall. The real-world consequences are serious: in the United States, mistaken eyewitness identification has been a factor in around 70% of convictions later overturned by DNA evidence. Biologically, stress hormones may affect how memories are stored, so a frightened witness may encode an event poorly."],
   ["Children as witnesses","Children are thought to be especially vulnerable. Research such as that of Ceci and Bruck suggests that young children are more suggestible, particularly when they are interviewed repeatedly or by an adult they want to please. They may give the answer they think is expected, and their schemas for events are less developed."],
   ["Evidence that EWT can be reliable","On the other hand, EWT can be accurate. Yuille and Cutshall (1986) interviewed 13 real witnesses to a shooting in Canada and found their accounts were highly accurate four to five months later, and they resisted misleading questions. This suggests lab studies like Loftus and Palmer lack ecological validity: students watching a film know nothing is at stake, whereas real witnesses are emotionally involved. Techniques such as the cognitive interview, which uses context reinstatement and reporting everything, increase accurate recall without increasing errors. Children can also give reliable accounts when they are interviewed carefully with open questions, as guidance in England and Wales now requires."],
   ["Implications","The debate has major implications. Ethically, unreliable testimony can lead to innocent people being imprisoned, and the guilty going free. Economically, wrongful convictions and appeals cost the justice system large sums, while training police in the cognitive interview and video-recording child interviews also require money and time. Socially, research has changed practice: judges give juries a warning about identification evidence (the Turnbull guidelines), and child witnesses can give evidence by video link to reduce stress."],
   ["Conclusion","In conclusion, EWT is not always unreliable, but it is fragile. Accuracy depends on factors such as the questions asked, the witness’s age and the stress of the event. The most useful approach is not to reject EWT but to collect it carefully, using non-leading questions and the cognitive interview, and to support it with other evidence such as CCTV or DNA."]]},
 {id:"positive",title:"Relevance of positive psychology in today’s society",sideA:"Positive psychology is highly relevant today",sideB:"Positive psychology has limited relevance today",
  core:"Positive psychology, launched by Seligman in 1998, studies well-being, strengths and what makes life worth living. The debate is whether it helps people in today’s society or oversimplifies their problems.",
  args:[["Mental health problems are rising, so prevention and well-being matter","A"],["Mindfulness-based cognitive therapy is recommended by NICE for recurrent depression","A"],["Myers and Diener (1995) found happiness depends more on relationships and outlook than wealth","A"],["Well-being programmes in schools and workplaces can build resilience","A"],["Wales places well-being in law and in the Curriculum for Wales","A"],
   ["It may blame individuals for unhappiness caused by poverty or discrimination","B"],["It reflects Western, individualistic values","B"],["Much research relies on self-report, which may be biased","B"],["Pressure to ‘stay positive’ can make people feel worse","B"],["Many findings are correlational, so cause and effect is unclear","B"]],
  imps:[["Telling people to think positively could ignore real suffering","eth"],["Interventions should not replace treatment for serious mental illness","eth"],["Self-report measures raise issues of honesty and privacy","eth"],
   ["Mindfulness and well-being programmes are cheap to deliver","eco"],["Happier employees may be more productive and take fewer sick days","eco"],["Prevention could reduce costs to the NHS","eco"],
   ["Schools teach well-being as part of the curriculum","soc"],["The Well-being of Future Generations (Wales) Act 2015 shapes public policy","soc"],["Could be used to make people accept poor conditions","soc"]],
  essay:[
   ["Introduction","Positive psychology was launched by Martin Seligman in 1998. Instead of focusing only on mental illness, it studies what makes people flourish, including positive emotions, strengths, relationships and meaning. It assumes that people have free will, that goodness and excellence are authentic, and that psychology should focus on ‘the good life’. This essay considers how relevant it is in today’s society."],
   ["Relevance to modern mental health","One argument for its relevance is that mental health problems, particularly among young people, are a major concern today, and traditional approaches mainly treat illness after it develops. Positive psychology aims to prevent problems by building resilience and well-being. Mindfulness, for example, teaches people to focus on the present without judgement, and mindfulness-based cognitive therapy is recommended by NICE to help prevent relapse in people with recurrent depression. Bangor University in Wales is home to a well-known centre for mindfulness research and training."],
   ["Research evidence","Myers and Diener (1995) reviewed research on subjective well-being and found that, above the poverty line, wealth is only weakly linked to happiness, while optimism, self-esteem, close relationships, meaningful work and faith are more important. This is relevant in a consumer society, where many people believe that money and possessions bring happiness."],
   ["Policy in Wales and the economy","Positive psychology has also influenced policy. The Well-being of Future Generations (Wales) Act 2015 requires public bodies to consider long-term well-being, and the Curriculum for Wales includes Health and Well-being as an Area of Learning and Experience. Economically, well-being programmes are relatively cheap and may reduce sickness absence and NHS costs, while happier employees may be more productive."],
   ["Criticisms","However, positive psychology has limitations. Critics argue that it can place responsibility for happiness on the individual and ignore social causes of unhappiness such as poverty, discrimination or poor housing. This has an ethical implication: telling someone in difficult circumstances to ‘think positively’ may increase guilt. It may also be used by organisations to make people accept poor working conditions rather than change them."],
   ["Methodological and cultural problems","There are also problems with the evidence. Much of it relies on self-report questionnaires, which are open to social desirability bias, and Myers and Diener’s findings are correlational, so it is not clear whether relationships cause happiness or happy people find relationships more easily. Positive psychology also reflects Western, individualistic values about personal achievement, so its ideas of ‘the good life’ may not apply in collectivist cultures. The biological approach would add that some differences in happiness are partly genetic, which limits how much can be changed by choice."],
   ["Conclusion","In conclusion, positive psychology is highly relevant today because it addresses well-being and prevention at a time of growing concern about mental health, and it is influencing education and policy in Wales. However, it should complement rather than replace treatments for mental illness, and it must take social and cultural factors into account so that it does not blame individuals for problems that are not of their making."]]}];
